[LESSON]: Can't use "if-then-else" directly in Alloy 6 facts or predicates → Must use implication (=>) or conditional expressions in let bindings.

[LESSON]: Alloy 6 does not have built-in time; time must be modeled explicitly as an abstract sig (Time) and indexed in state.

[LESSON]: Can't set field values for all states in a fact directly; must use quantification and implications over State and Time.

[LESSON]: Alloy does not support Boolean values (True/False) as first-class types; must model flag fields explicitly as enums or signatures if needed.

[LESSON]: Alloy's set operations (&, +, -) apply to sets only; multiplicity must be respected (e.g., lone, one, some, set).

[LESSON]: Alloy doesn't support method-like function application with arguments in all contexts; must use relational join and comprehension.

[LESSON]: Can't refer to "previous" or "next" State directly without explicit temporal modeling; must use explicit Time or State sequence.

[LESSON]: To denote that a property holds for a duration, must use quantification over all relevant States and Times.

[LESSON]: For predicates representing requirements, must use explicit composition of previous predicates for incremental verification.
